    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nDocument-level info:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 6\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 3489487\n- Page total: 11\n- Member state: Netherlands\n- Date issued: 20 May 2022\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Address lines: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\nApplicants:\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\nManufacturers: same as applicant — ABB Limited.\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: Water meter (electromagnetic water meter)\n- Type designations: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z, FET4*\n  - X = 1 or 3, Y = 1, 2, 8 or 9, Z = R, V or F\n- Description: An electromagnetic water meter...\n\nModel family: The cert covers a family with two type bases: FEW4XY.Z (flow sensor presumably) and FET4 (transmitter?). Actually the types are \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". With X being 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only), Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\n\nThe family of instruments varies along:\n- Bore: reduced bore vs (virtual) full bore\n- Power: AC mains vs battery/renewable energy\n- Meter size: DN40 ... DN1800\n- Accuracy class: 1 and 2\n\nTables 2–5 are flow rate tables by meter size and accuracy class, split by bore type and power supply. Tables 6–9 are indicating device characteristics by meter size, also split by bore and power.\n\nFor model_level characteristics, there are a LOT of model variants. I should extract matrix_tables for Tables 2-5 and 6-9, and populate model_family models and characteristics.model_level.\n\nThe schema says matrix_tables should preserve multi-dim tables. Given the complexity, I'll:\n- Define config axes: bore (reduced/full), power_supply (AC mains / battery-renewable)\n- Models: meter sizes DN40...DN1800\n\nHmm, but the model matrix depends on both accuracy class and config (bore × power). The model_level entries have attribute, unit, values [{model, value}]. The model could be a compound id like \"DN40/reduced/AC/class 1\".\n\nGiven the huge number of rows, I need to decide how much to include. The rules say: matrix_tables[]: preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. And populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level.\n\nLet me build the matrix_tables carefully:\n\nTable 2: Reduced bore - AC mains powered\nColumns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1\nUnits: null, null, m³/h, m³/h, m³/h, m³/h, null\n\nRows:\n- DN40, class 1: 0.05, 0.08, 25, 31.25, 500\n- DN40, class 2: 0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000\n- DN50, class 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, class 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n- DN100, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN125, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN125, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN150, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n- DN200, 1&2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n- DN250, 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n- DN300, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN350, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN350, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN400, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN450, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN500, 1: 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500\n- DN500, 2: 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000\n- DN600, 1: 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500\n- DN600, 2: 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000\n\nTable 3: Reduced bore - Battery/Renewable energy powered\nRows:\n- DN40, 1&2: 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n- DN50, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n- DN65, 1: 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\n- DN65, 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n- DN80, 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n- DN80, 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- DN100, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN100, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN125, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN125, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN150, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n- DN200, 1&2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n- DN250, 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n- DN250, 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n- DN300, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN300, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n- DN350, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN350, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n- DN400, 1: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN400, 2: 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n- DN450, 1: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN450, 2: 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n- DN500, 1: 100, 160, 4000, 5000, 40\n(page 5 continues Table 3:)\n- DN500, 2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n- DN600, 1: 157.5, 252, 6300, 7875, 40\n- DN600, 2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\nTable 4: Full bore - AC mains powered\n- DN40, 1&2: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, 1&2: 0.126, 0.2016, 63, 78.75, 500\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.2, 0.32, 100, 125, 500\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, 1&2: 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\n- DN125, 1&2: 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\n- DN150, 1: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN150, 2: 1.26, 2.016, 630, 787.5, 500\n- DN200, 1: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n- DN200, 2: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, 1: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN250, 2: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, 1: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN300, 2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN350, 1&2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n- DN400, 1&2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n(page 6 continues Table 4:)\n- DN450, 1&2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n- DN500, 1&2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n- DN600, 1&2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n- DN700, 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN700, 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\n- DN750, 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN750, 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\n- DN800, 1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN800, 2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200\n- DN900, 1: 158.7, 253.9, 10000, 12500, 63\n- DN900, 2: 80, 128, 10000, 12500, 125\n- DN1000, 1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1000, 2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\n- DN1050, 1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1050, 2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200\n- DN1100, 1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1100, 2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\n- DN1200, 1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1200, 2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125\n- DN1350, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1350, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1400, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1400, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1500, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1500, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1600, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1600, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n- DN1650, 1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1650, 2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80\n(page 7 continues Table 4:)\n- DN1800, 1&2: 317.5, 507.9, 16000, 20000, 50\n\nTable 5: Full bore - Battery/Renewable energy powered\n- DN40, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 40, 50, 125\n- DN40, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 40, 50, 250\n- DN50, 1: 0.504, 0.8064, 63, 78.75, 125\n- DN50, 2: 0.252, 0.4032, 63, 78.75, 250\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250\n- DN100, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN125, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN150, 1: 5.04, 8.064, 630, 787.5, 125\n- DN150, 2: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN200, 1: 8, 12.8, 1000, 1250, 125\n- DN200, 2: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n- DN250, 1: 12.8, 20.48, 1600, 2000, 125\n- DN250, 2: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN300, 1: 20, 32, 2500, 3125, 125\n- DN300, 2: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN350, 1&2: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN400, 1&2: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n(page 8 continues Table 5:)\n- DN450, 1&2: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN500, 1&2: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN600, 1: 78.75, 126, 6300, 7875, 80\n- DN600, 2: 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN700, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN700, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN750, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN750, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN800, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n- DN800, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n- DN900, 2: 158.7, 254, 10000, 12500, 63\n- DN1000, 2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1050, 2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n- DN1100, 2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1200, 2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n- DN1350, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1400, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1500, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1600, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n- DN1650, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n\nTables 6-9: indicating device characteristics.\n\nTable 6: Reduced bore – AC mains powered (indicating device)\nColumns: meter_size, indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max (all in m³)\n- DN40, DN50, DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN80: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN250: 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\n- DN300-DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nTable 7: Reduced bore – Battery/Renewable\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 8: Full bore – AC mains powered\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700-DN1200: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN1350-DN1800: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nTable 9: Full bore – Battery/Renewable\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN200-DN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN600: 99999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN900-DN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nThis is a very large certificate. The output JSON could get massive. I need to balance completeness with practicality. The schema says to preserve tables in matrix_tables and populate model_level. Given the token constraints, I'll include the matrix tables fully but for model_level I might select the key attributes.\n\nActually, let me think about how to structure models. The models could be defined by combinations. One approach: model_id like \"FEW4XY.Z DN40 reduced bore AC mains class 1\". That's overly complex.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach:\n- model_family.family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: list of meter sizes? Or of the two sensor types?\n\nThe type designations: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z (flow sensor) and FET4* — actually FET4 is the transmitter? \"FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\" with X, Y, Z defined. Hmm, actually AquaMaster4 is the family; FEW4XY.Z is the flow sensor designation; FET4 is... let me re-read: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\" — \"* With X being 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only) and Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n\nSo the type is AquaMaster4, with designations FEW4XY.Z and FET4. The asterisk applies to both, defining wildcards X, Y, Z.\n\nGiven complexity, the practical approach:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: I'll enumerate the meter sizes as model variants, since the flow characteristics vary per size and class. But class also varies...\n\nMaybe simplest: models = the distinct meter sizes DN40...DN1800, and use model_level for q3 (permanent flow rate) per size, and put class-dependent values into matrix_tables.\n\nActually, the model_level characteristic values[] include {model, value}. If for a given size there are two values (class 1 and class 2), I can't put both under one model entry. So better to define models as size+class combos? That's ~100 entries. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, models could be size×config combos, and model_level attribute per model.\n\nGiven token budget, I think a reasonable approach:\n1. Include full matrix_tables for Tables 2-9 (the primary data).\n2. model_family.models: enumerate meter sizes with attributes {bore_options, power_options, accuracy_classes}? The schema says attributes{} is free-form.\n3. characteristics.model_level: include a few key attributes like q3 (permanent flow rate) per size for AC mains... but that duplicates tables.\n\nActually rule 4 says for the R137 example: extract as matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level. For this huge cert, doing both fully would be enormous. I'll do:\n\n- matrix_tables: all 8 tables fully (Tables 2-9).\n- model_family.models: meter size entries (the family axis), with attributes noting applicable classes and power options.\n- model_level: I'll include the q3 permanent flow rate per size+class+config as compound model ids? That would duplicate matrix tables.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. Maybe define models as the size-class combos with a compound model_id like \"DN40|reduced bore|AC mains|class 1\". Then model_level entries for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio per model. That's the full duplication of the tables — too much.\n\nCompromise: Include model_level for the most meaningful attribute — permanent flow rate Q3 — with model ids like \"DN40 reduced bore AC mains class 1\"? That's 100 entries. Still large.\n\nAlternative: use config_level for the two axes (bore, power supply) and model_level keyed on size only for Q3, plus matrix_tables for the full data. For class-dependent variation, note in config or just keep tables.\n\nLet me think about what's most useful and schema-compliant:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- attribute: flow_rate_q3, axis: \"power_supply\", values: [{condition: \"reduced bore, AC mains\", ...}] — no, config_level values are {condition, value}.\n\nActually config axes here: bore (R/V/F — note R vs V/F differ in pressure loss and flow ranges partially), power supply (AC vs battery), accuracy class (1 vs 2). The pressure loss class varies by bore and size:\n- Reduced bore: DN40/DN50: Δp 40 (0.40 bar); larger than DN50: Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\n- (Virtual) Full bore: DN40/DN50: Δp 10 (0.10 bar); DN65-DN200: Δp 16 (0.16 bar); DN250+: Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\n\nFlow profile sensitivity: U0/D0 except full bore DN250+: U3/D0.\n\nI could use config_level for pressure loss class:\n- attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: bore, values: [{condition: \"reduced bore, DN40-DN50\", value: \"Δp 40\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore, >DN50\", value: \"Δp 63\"}, {condition: \"full bore, DN40-DN50\", value: \"Δp 10\"}, {condition: \"full bore, DN65-DN200\", value: \"Δp 16\"}, {condition: \"full bore, DN250+\", value: \"Δp 10\"}]\n\nSimilarly for flow profile sensitivity class.\n\nType-level characteristics (Table 1):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" (string or list) — keep as string \"1 and 2\"? The recommendation.accuracy_classes is [1, 2]. For characteristics, value could be \"1, 2\" string. I'll use \"1 and 2\" as string.\n- environmental_class: \"O\" with note \"installed outdoors\" — value: \"O (installed outdoors)\" as string.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\" with range +0.1 to +50 °C. Value: \"T50\" string; maybe add water_temperature_range as {min 0.1, max 50} °C. The table says \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\". I'll record water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (16 bar). Record maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa; plus note. Maybe also max_admissible_pressure_bar: 16. I'll do value 1.6, unit MPa, and a second characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_alt: 16 bar? Better: two entries: maximum_admissible_pressure (MPa) and maximum_admissible_pressure_bar (bar). Or keep the \"(16 bar)\" as footnote-like. I'll include both as separate characteristics.\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\" — string.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have: U3 and D0\" — this is config-dependent; put in config_level with axis \"bore_and_size\".\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — boolean true? Keep as string.\n- pressure_loss_class: config_level as discussed.\n- power_supply: three options:\n  - AC mains: 85–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz → power_supply_voltage_ac: {min 85, max 240} V; power_supply_frequency: {min 50, max 60} Hz; power_supply_type: \"AC mains\"\n  - Renewable energy DC: 6–32 V → power_supply_voltage_dc: {min 6, max 32} V\n  - Replaceable battery: 2x 3.6 V (operating range 2.9–3.6 V) → battery_voltage_nominal: 3.6 V (2 cells); battery_voltage_operating_range: {min 2.9, max 3.6} V; battery_cells: 2\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" — string.\n- software identification: three versions with checksums. Record as software_versions list? The schema has no explicit place. Could put in type_level as software_identification string, or list each version. I'll add type_level: software_identification with value string listing all, or better an array? Value must be number|minmax|string. I'll make three characteristics? Perhaps software_identification: \"03.00.03 (B26AFE6B); 03.00.04 (6E98963A); 03.02.00 (E12E5C04)\". Keep one string.\n- documentation file: T11851-5 → test_reports[] with role documentation_file.\n\nComponents: The cert describes flow sensor and flow transmitter? \"Temperature range ambient ... (for both flow sensor and flow transmitter)\". Type designations FEW4XY.Z (sensor) and FET4 (transmitter?). Actually in AquaMaster4, FEW4 is the flow sensor and FET4 is the flow transmitter (remote version). Y being 1,2,8,9 = integral/remote version, optionally sensor only. So components:\n- role: flow_sensor, type_designations: [\"FEW4XY.Z\"], characteristics: bore options Z = R/V/F\n- role: flow_transmitter (indicating device), type_designations: [\"FET4\"]\n\nHmm, but \"FET4 *\" — the wildcard definitions apply to both? X (1 or 3 standard/advanced) probably refers to transmitter version. Y (integral/remote, sensor only) refers to pairing. Z (R/V/F) refers to sensor bore.\n\nI'll record components with these roles, noting it's inferred. To be safe, I'll include components with roles \"flow sensor\" and \"flow transmitter (indicating device)\". Actually the cert explicitly mentions \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\" in the temperature row, so it's grounded.\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-2254799-01, 2020-08-14, 235 pages, role type_evaluation_report\n- NMi-2254799-03, 2020-09-30, 44 pages\n- NMi-2254799-04, 2020-12-24, 180 pages\n- NMi-2564429-01, 2021-08-02, 26 pages\n- NMi-2496280-01, 2021-08-26, 29 pages\n- NMi-2496280-02, 2021-11-23, 25 pages\n- Documentation folder T11851-5, role documentation_file\n\nDates: format? Use ISO \"2020-08-14\" probably good. certificate.date_issued: \"2022-05-20\".\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\" (canonical). The rule example: \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". So id: \"R49\", edition: 1? The recommendation is \"R 49-1 (2013)\". R49-1 is part 1. Edition: 2013? Schema says edition (int). Hmm — 2013 is the year, not edition number. R49-1:2013 — I'd put edition: 2013? Or 1? The example \"R60\" edition would be 2000 edition etc. For R49-1 (2013), the edition year is 2013. I'll set edition: 2013 and amendment: null, and maybe include part info in... there's no field for part. I could set id: \"R49-1\"? Canonical example says \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". R49-1 is the standard way to refer to part 1. Hmm. The recommendation given: R 49-1 (2013). I'll use id \"R49-1\", edition 2013. Actually \"edition (int)\" — for OIML, the year serves as edition. R60 2000 edition → edition 2000. So edition: 2013 works.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2]\n\nRevision history:\n- Initial, 2020-08-14, \"First issue.\"\n- 1, 2020-09-30, ...\n- 2, 2020-12-24, ...\n- 3, 2021-08-02, ...\n- 4, 2021-08-26, ...\n- 5, 2021-11-23, ...\n- 6, 2022-05-20, ...\n\nFootnotes: none defined in the cert. The \"Please note\" bullets are notes, not footnotes. I could add them as footnotes? They don't have markers. Skip, or include as descriptions on matrix tables. I'll include the note text in matrix_tables description (abbreviated).\n\nModel family: I'll set family_name \"AquaMaster4\" and models as the distinct meter sizes with attributes {accuracy_classes, bore, power} — hmm, per-size the classes available vary by table. That's the table data. To keep models simple: models = list of {model_id: \"DN40\"} ... {model_id: \"DN1800\"} — 40+ sizes. Sizes: reduced bore: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 (15). Full bore: DN40–DN1800: DN40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350, 400, 450, 500, 600, 700, 750, 800, 900, 1000, 1050, 1100, 1200, 1350, 1400, 1500, 1600, 1650, 1800 (29). Union: 29 distinct sizes (full bore includes all reduced sizes? Reduced max DN600; full bore covers DN40–DN1800 including 600). Union = 29 sizes.\n\nFor model_level characteristics, I'll extract the permanent flow rate Q3 per size... but Q3 is same across power supply for a given size/bore? Check: DN40 reduced AC: Q3 25; reduced battery: 25. DN65 reduced AC: 63; battery: 63. DN100 reduced AC: 160; battery: 160. So Q3 depends only on bore+size, not power. And bore: DN40 reduced: 25; full: 40. So Q3 varies by (bore, size). And class doesn't change Q3 (only Q1/Q2/ratio).\n\nSo model_level could be:\n- attribute: permanent_flow_rate_q3, axis by model \"DNxx (reduced bore)\" and \"DNxx (full bore)\". That's 15 + 29 = 44 entries. Large but doable. Plus q4 = 1.25 × q3 always — could compute but don't fabricate; it's in tables.\n\nHmm, token budget. The matrix_tables will already be huge (Tables 2-5: 25+26+44+38 ≈ 133 rows; Tables 6-9: ~30 rows total). That's a lot of JSON. Each row object with 7 keys... roughly 133 × ~120 chars ≈ 16KB just for tables 2-5. Plus tables 6-9. Plus everything else. Probably 25-30KB total output. That should be OK within limits.\n\nShould I also do model_level duplication of Q1-Q4? That would double size. Rule 4's example does populate both, but for a table this big, the matrix_tables capture everything. I'll add model_level for a modest subset: q3 per bore+size? Actually, to comply with the spirit, maybe skip model_level duplication and rely on matrix_tables, since rule 4 says \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — these tables DO fit in matrix_tables. The R137 example populated model_level because the table was small. For practicality, I'll include matrix_tables fully and a small model_level with q3 (the key rating parameter) keyed by bore+size compound model.\n\nHmm, but the instruction explicitly says for R137-like 2-D tables: extract as matrix_tables AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level. To honor that without exploding, I'll:\n\n- model_family.models: 29 size entries (model_id \"DN40\"...\"DN1800\").\n- characteristics.model_level: attribute \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\" with values per model+condition where condition distinguishes bore: model: \"DN40 (reduced bore)\", value 25; \"DN40 (full bore)\", value 40; etc. 44 values. And attribute \"overload_flow_rate_q4\" similarly? That's another 44. Maybe just q3 and q4? Or just q3. I'll do q3 only, since q4 = 1.25·q3 and it's in tables.\n\nWait — check q4: all tables show q4 = 1.25 × q3 indeed. Yes.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: model: \"DN40 (reduced bore)\" — is \"model\" the right key? The schema: values[]: {model, value}. I can use model ids like \"DN40-R\" / \"DN40-F\"? Better keep readable: \"DN40 (reduced bore)\".\n\nAlternatively define models as size+bore combos: 44 models. Then model_level q3 values: 44 entries. And attributes on models could note classes available per power. That's cleaner: model_family.models = 44 entries {model_id: \"DN40 reduced bore\", attributes: {sizes...}}? Hmm, 44 model entries with attributes could be big too. Let me keep models as 29 sizes and note in family description.\n\nLet me now also handle config_level:\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class, axis \"bore_and_size\": values: [{condition: \"reduced bore and full bore up to DN200\", value: \"U0/D0\"}, {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3/D0\"}]. The original text: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have: U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". So: reduced bore all sizes: U0/D0; full bore DN40–DN200: U0/D0; full bore DN250+: U3/D0.\n- pressure_loss_class, axis \"bore_and_size\": values as listed.\n\nNow the matrix_tables. Columns naming: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_min, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1.\n\nFor Tables 6-9: columns: meter_size, indicating_range_max_m3, indicating_range_min_m3, verification_scale_interval_max_m3. Meter sizes are grouped in cells (\"DN40, DN50, DN65\"). I'll keep the grouped string as meter_size value.\n\nUnits: m³/h for flows; m³ for indicating range.\n\nNow write all rows.\n\nTable 2 rows (reduced bore, AC mains):\n1. DN40, 1: 0.05, 0.08, 25, 31.25, 500\n2. DN40, 2: 0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000\n3. DN50, 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n4. DN50, 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n5. DN65, \"1 & 2\": 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n6. DN80, \"1 & 2\": 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n7. DN100, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n8. DN100, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n9. DN125, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n10. DN125, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n11. DN150, \"1 & 2\": 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n12. DN200, \"1 & 2\": 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n13. DN250, 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n14. DN250, 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n15. DN300, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n16. DN300, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n17. DN350, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n18. DN350, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n19. DN400, \"1 & 2\": 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n20. DN450, \"1 & 2\": 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n21. DN500, 1: 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500\n22. DN500, 2: 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000\n23. DN600, 1: 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500\n24. DN600, 2: 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000\n\nTable 3 rows (reduced bore, battery/renewable):\n1. DN40, \"1 & 2\": 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n2. DN50, \"1 & 2\": 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n3. DN65, 1: 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\n4. DN65, 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n5. DN80, 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n6. DN80, 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n7. DN100, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n8. DN100, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n9. DN125, 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n10. DN125, 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n11. DN150, \"1 & 2\": 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n12. DN200, \"1 & 2\": 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n13. DN250, 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n14. DN250, 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n15. DN300, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n16. DN300, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n17. DN350, 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n18. DN350, 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n19. DN400, 1: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n20. DN400, 2: 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n21. DN450, 1: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n22. DN450, 2: 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n23. DN500, 1: 100, 160, 4000, 5000, 40\n24. DN500, 2: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n25. DN600, 1: 157.5, 252, 6300, 7875, 40\n26. DN600, 2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\nTable 4 rows (full bore, AC mains):\n1. DN40, \"1 & 2\": 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n2. DN50, \"1 & 2\": 0.126, 0.2016, 63, 78.75, 500\n3. DN65, \"1 & 2\": 0.2, 0.32, 100, 125, 500\n4. DN80, \"1 & 2\": 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n5. DN100, \"1 & 2\": 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\n6. DN125, \"1 & 2\": 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500\n7. DN150, 1: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n8. DN150, 2: 1.26, 2.016, 630, 787.5, 500\n9. DN200, 1: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n10. DN200, 2: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n11. DN250, 1: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n12. DN250, 2: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n13. DN300, 1: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n14. DN300, 2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n15. DN350, \"1 & 2\": 40, 