00, 125\n14. DN250, 2: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n15. DN300, 1: 20, 32, 2500, 3125, 125\n16. DN300, 2: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n17. DN350, \"1 & 2\": 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n18. DN400, \"1 & 2\": 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n19. DN450, \"1 & 2\": 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n20. DN500, \"1 & 2\": 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n21. DN600, 1: 78.75, 126, 6300, 7875, 80\n22. DN600, 2: 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n23. DN700, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n24. DN700, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n25. DN750, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n26. DN750, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n27. DN800, 1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n28. DN800, 2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n29. DN900, 2: 158.7, 254, 10000, 12500, 63\n30. DN1000, 2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n31. DN1050, 2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n32. DN1100, 2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n33. DN1200, 2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n34. DN1350, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n35. DN1400, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n36. DN1500, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n37. DN1600, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n38. DN1650, 2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n\nTables 6–9 rows (indicating device). Columns: meter_size, indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max; units m³.\n\nTable 6 (reduced bore, AC mains):\n- \"DN40, DN50, DN65\": 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- \"DN80\": 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- \"DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200\": 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- \"DN250\": 9999999, 9999999, 0.001\n- \"DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600\": 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n\nTable 7 (reduced bore, battery/renewable):\n- \"DN40, DN50\": 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- \"DN65\": 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- \"DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150\": 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- \"DN200\": 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- \"DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450\": 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- \"DN500, DN600\": 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 8 (full bore, AC mains):\n- \"DN40, DN50\": 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- \"DN65\": 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- \"DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150\": 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- \"DN200, DN250, DN300\": 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- \"DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600\": 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- \"DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200\": 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- \"DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800\": 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nTable 9 (full bore, battery/renewable):\n- \"DN40, DN50\": 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- \"DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125\": 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- \"DN150\": 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- \"DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500\": 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- \"DN600\": 99999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- \"DN700, DN750, DN800\": 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- \"DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650\": 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nNow model_level: I'll add permanent_flow_rate_q3 keyed by size+bore (since it's independent of power supply):\n\nReduced bore: DN40: 25; DN50: 40; DN65: 63; DN80: 100; DN100: 160; DN125: 160; DN150: 400; DN200: 630; DN250: 1000; DN300: 1600; DN350: 1600; DN400: 2500; DN450: 2500; DN500: 4000; DN600: 6300.\nFull bore: DN40: 40; DN50: 63; DN65: 100; DN80: 160; DN100: 250; DN125: 250; DN150: 630; DN200: 1000; DN250: 1600; DN300: 2500; DN350: 4000; DN400: 4000; DN450: 6300; DN500: 6300; DN600: 6300; DN700: 10000; DN750: 10000; DN800: 10000; DN900: 10000; DN1000: 16000; DN1050: 16000; DN1100: 16000; DN1200: 16000; DN1350: 16000; DN1400: 16000; DN1500: 16000; DN1600: 16000; DN1650: 16000; DN1800: 16000.\n\nThat's 44 values. Unit m³/h.\n\nAlso overload_flow_rate_q4? It's exactly 1.25×q3 everywhere. Could include as another 44-entry attribute. To save space, include it? The instructions emphasize not fabricating but q4 is explicitly in tables. I'll include only q3 in model_level to keep size manageable, plus ratio? Ratio varies by class and power. Keep matrix_tables as source of truth.\n\nAlso, should model ids in model_level match model_family.models? If models are \"DN40\"...\"DN1800\", then values entries use model: \"DN40 (reduced bore)\" which doesn't match. Alternative: define models as bore+size combos: \"DN40 reduced bore\", ... That's 44 models. Hmm, or keep models as sizes and use config \"bore\" for the q3 distinction via config_level:\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute: permanent_flow_rate_q3, axis: \"bore\", values: [{condition: \"reduced bore\", value: {list}}] — no, values are {condition, value} single values.\n\nOK simplest compliant: model_family.models = 44 entries combining size+bore? That's big. Or 29 sizes with attributes {bore: [\"reduced\", \"full\"], ...}.\n\nI think the cleanest: models = size entries (29), and model_level q3 with model keys \"DN40 (reduced bore)\" etc. The model key mismatch is acceptable since the \"model\" field is a reference label.\n\nAlternatively, skip separate q3 model_level and instead just have matrix tables. But rule 4 says populate model_level. I'll include the q3 model_level with bore-qualified model ids, and keep model_family.models as bore-qualified too for consistency: models = 44 entries {model_id: \"DN40 reduced bore\"} etc. Hmm 44 entries × ~40 chars = manageable (~2KB).\n\nActually wait — for reduced bore, sizes go DN40–DN600 (15 sizes); full bore DN40–DN1800 (29 sizes). Total 44. Let me enumerate reduced bore sizes: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 — 15. Full bore: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600, DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800 — 29. Total 44.\n\n44 model entries plus 44 q3 values — fine.\n\nI'll add attributes to models? E.g., {\"model_id\": \"DN40 reduced bore\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_classes\": [1,2]}} — but classes depend on power supply (e.g., DN40 reduced AC: class 1 and 2 with different ratios; battery: 1&2 same row). Too fine-grained; skip attributes or minimal.\n\nComponents: I'll include:\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FEW4XY.Z\"], characteristics: null} — hmm, \"FEW4XY.Z\" with wildcards. Also alternatives for Z (R/V/F)? That's a model wildcard not alternatives. I'll put notes in description? components[].characteristics is {} free-form. I'll add {\"bore_options\": [\"R (reduced bore)\", \"V (virtual full bore)\", \"F (full bore)\"]}.\n- {role: \"flow transmitter / indicating device\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"], characteristics: {\"version_options\": [\"1 (standard)\", \"3 (advanced)\"]}}.\n\nWait — X (1 or 3 standard/advanced): does X apply to sensor or transmitter? For AquaMaster4, FEW4 is sensor; FET4 transmitter. X could be sensor electronics version. Ambiguous. I'll avoid attributing X specifically; put wildcard explanation in certified_type.description.\n\ncertified_type.description: \"Electromagnetic water meter AquaMaster4, types FEW4XY.Z and FET4, where X is 1 or 3 (standard or advanced), Y is 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only), and Z is R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n\nmodule_designation: not applicable — the schema says \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. This is a water meter, not a module cert. I'd use \"Not applicable\" or null. I'll use null... Actually \"Not applicable\" seems right since it's a complete instrument certificate. I'll put \"Not applicable\".\n\nCertificate number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" with revision 6. The field number — include revision? Schema has number, and revision_history. I'll put number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and maybe include revision in a field? There's no revision field at cert level; revision_history covers it, and the current revision is \"6\". Hmm, certificate.number could be \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and I could add revision \"6\"? Schema doesn't list it; rule 8 says omit if not in schema. But it's useful... I'll keep number as-is without revision; revision_history captures rev 6. Actually maybe include \"revision\": \"6\" — schema says don't fabricate, but adding extra keys is riskier for schema validation. I'll stick to schema fields.\n\npage_total: 11.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" — or name \"NMi Certin B.V.\" with oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\". I'll use name \"NMi Certin B.V.\" and oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\".\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: \"Certification Board\" — hmm, that's the board, not his title. Omit person_title. Actually \"Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" — no title given. Omit.\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n\ncertificate.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\ndate_issued: \"2022-05-20\".\n\nType designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]. Or list [\"FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4\"] with family AquaMaster4. I'll use type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]. Hmm, the original: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". I'll go with [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"].\n\ncategory: \"Water meter\".\n\ntest_reports dates ISO:\n- NMi-2254799-01: 2020-08-14, 235 pages, type_evaluation_report\n- NMi-2254799-03: 2020-09-30, 44, type_evaluation_report\n- NMi-2254799-04: 2020-12-24, 180, type_evaluation_report\n- NMi-2564429-01: 2021-08-02, 26, type_evaluation_report\n- NMi-2496280-01: 2021-08-26, 29, type_evaluation_report\n- NMi-2496280-02: 2021-11-23, 25, type_evaluation_report\n- T11851-5: documentation_file, no date/pages.\n\nRole: the cert says \"results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\" — these are Type Evaluation Report parts. Role \"type_evaluation_report\" fits.\n\nProduction locations: extra manufacturers? Page 11 lists production locations: ABB Limited (UK), ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd. (China), ABB Inc. (USA), ABB India Limited (India). These are manufacturing/production locations. I'll include them in manufacturers[] since they are production locations of the certified type. That seems appropriate — manufacturers[] entries with name and address_lines.\n\nCharacteristics type_level values — structured objects:\n\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E2\"}\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: also 16 bar — I'll add maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}. Hmm, or just one characteristic with unit MPa and note. I'll include both.\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"}\n- reverse_flow: {value: true}? The text: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → value true is fine, or the string. I'll use true.\n- checking_facilities: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — hmm; recommendation already has accuracy_classes [1,2]. Keep in type_level as string \"1 and 2\".\n- software identification: I'll add three entries? Or one: software_identification: {value: \"03.00.03 (checksum B26AFE6B); 03.00.04 (checksum 6E98963A); 03.02.00 (checksum E12E5C04)\"}. Keep single string.\n- power supply entries:\n  - power_supply_type: {value: \"AC mains, renewable energy (DC), replaceable battery\"} — better split:\n  - ac_mains_voltage: {value: {min: 85, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - power_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n  - dc_supply_voltage: {value: {min: 6, max: 32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - battery_cell_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"} with note 2 cells — add battery_configuration: {value: \"2 cells in series\"}? \"2x 3,6 V\" — battery_cells: {value: 2}. operating range battery_voltage_operating_range: {min 2.9, max 3.6} V.\n\nConfig-level:\n- pressure_loss_class (axis: bore/size): values with condition strings and value strings \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\" etc. Use structured? config values: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. The pressure loss class: value \"Δp 40\" plus 0.40 bar. I'll set value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\" as string. Conditions:\n  - \"reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\": \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"\n  - \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\": \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"\n  - \"full bore or virtual full bore, DN40 and DN50\": \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"\n  - \"full bore or virtual full bore, DN65 to DN200\": \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"\n  - \"full bore or virtual full bore, DN250 and larger\": \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class (axis: bore/size):\n  - \"reduced bore, all sizes\": \"U0/D0\"\n  - \"full bore, up to DN200\": \"U0/D0\"\n  - \"full bore, DN250 and larger\": \"U3/D0\"\n  With description note: 0×DN upstream/downstream for U0/D0; 3×DN upstream for U3.\n\nNow, matrix_tables entries: I'll produce 8 tables. Each with name, columns, column_units, rows, description, footnotes.\n\nThe \"Please note\" text appears after Tables 2/3/4/5 — I'll put a condensed description on tables 2-5: \"Flow rates Q1, Q2 are minimum values with Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3, Q4 are maximum values with Q4/Q3 = 1.25; ratio Q3/Q1 at least 40; values selectable per OIML R49-1:2013 §4.1.\" as description. Not footnotes (no markers).\n\nUnits: m³/h for flows; m³ for indicating range.\n\nNow model_level: attribute \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", unit \"m³/h\", values list with model ids \"DN40 reduced bore\" etc. 44 entries.\n\nLet me also double check DN600 reduced AC mains: Q1 12.6 — yes \"12,6\". Q2 20.16. OK.\n\nCheck Table 5 DN600 class 1: Q1 78.75, Q2 126, ratio 80. 78.75 × 80 = 6300 ✓.\n\nAlso Table 3 DN500 class1: Q1 100 → ratio 40 (4000/100=40 ✓).\n\nNow revision_history: revision as string (\"Initial\", \"1\"...\"6\"). Dates ISO:\n- Initial: 2020-08-14\n- 1: 2020-09-30\n- 2: 2020-12-24\n- 3: 2021-08-02\n- 4: 2021-08-26\n- 5: 2021-11-23\n- 6: 2022-05-20\n\nChanges: as given.\n\nFootnotes: none — omit footnotes array (rule 8: omit if not present).\n\nOutput size concern: matrix tables have ~133+30 rows. Each row ~110 chars → ~18KB. Acceptable.\n\nLet me also decide on \"meter_size\" column values like \"DN40\". accuracy_class values: use string \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\". Or numbers where single. I'll use strings consistently: \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\".\n\nColumns for flow tables: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1_minimum\", \"q2_transitional\", \"q3_permanent\", \"q4_overload\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"], column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null].\n\nFor indicating device tables: columns [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_max\", \"indicating_range_min\", \"verification_scale_interval_max\"], units [null, \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"].\n\nLet me write the JSON now. I need to be careful with decimal conversion: European commas → dots.\n\nAlso \"Temperature range ambient\" applies to both sensor and transmitter — noted.\n\nLet me assemble the final JSON. I'll aim for correctness and completeness while keeping reasonable size.\n\nFor model_family:\n```json\n\"model_family\": {\n  \"family_name\": \"AquaMaster4\",\n  \"models\": [ {\"model_id\": \"DN40 reduced bore\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN50 reduced bore\"}, ..., {\"model_id\": \"DN1800 full bore\"} ]\n}\n```\n44 entries.\n\nThen model_level q3 with matching model ids.\n\nActually also add model attribute? Each model could carry attributes {\"accuracy_classes\": \"1 and 2\"} — all sizes have both classes available across configs. Skip.\n\nLet me finalize component entries:\n\ncomponents:\n[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"flow sensor\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"FEW4XY.Z\"],\n    \"alternatives\": null,\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"bore_variants\": {\"R\": \"reduced bore\", \"V\": \"virtual full bore\", \"F\": \"full bore\"},\n      \"version_variants\": {\"1\": \"standard\", \"3\": \"advanced\"},\n      \"mounting_variants\": {\"Y=1,2,8,9\": \"integral/remote version, optionally sensor only\"}\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"flow transmitter (indicating device)\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"FET4\"],\n    \"alternatives\": null,\n    \"characteristics\": {}\n  }\n]\n\nHmm, the FET4 might be the transmitter used in remote versions. The cert text: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". It's ambiguous which wildcards apply to which. I'll keep characteristics minimal: for sensor, bore variants Z; note X, Y, Z wildcards in description instead. Let me simplify: components with roles and type designations only, plus characteristics where confident (Z bore for sensor).\n\nActually Y = integral/remote/sensor-only describes configuration between sensor and transmitter. I'll put that in description.\n\nPower supply note \"AC mains: 85 –240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz\" — voltage 85–240 V. Good.\n\nOne more thing: \"Replaceable Battery: 2x 3,6 V (operating range: 2,9 - 3,6 V)\" — battery_voltage 3.6 V per cell, 2 cells → total 7.2 V. Record battery_voltage_per_cell 3.6 V, battery_cells 2, battery_operating_range 2.9–3.6 V (per cell, presumably). I'll label carefully.\n\nOK, writing final JSON. I'll be careful that it's valid JSON — no trailing commas.\n\nAlso, note that the certificate page 2 Table 1 \"Checking facilities\" appears at top of page 3 as a table continuation — value \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\".\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49-1\"? The example format says canonical e.g. \"R60\". R49-1 is part 1 of R49. I'll use \"R49-1\" to be precise. edition: 2013. scheme \"A\". accuracy_classes [1, 2]. Hmm, maybe amendment null — omit.\n\ncertificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Should I append \" revision 6\"? I'll leave base number; add \"revision\": \"6\"? Not in schema. The revision_history has it. I'll skip.\n\nActually, let me include it in number? No — keep clean.\n\nLet me now carefully write everything out.\n\nFor the description fields of tables, include the note about freely chosen flow rates.\n\nFinal check on some numbers:\n- Table 2 DN600 class1: Q1 12,6 → 12.6; Q2 20,16 → 20.16 ✓\n- Table 4 DN900 class1: 158.7, 253.9 ✓; class2: 80, 128 ✓\n- Table 8 rows ✓.\n\nTable 9 last row: \"DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650\": max 999999999, min 99999999, VSI 1 ✓.\n\nNow writing the JSON. I'll write it compactly but readable.\n\nOne consideration: JSON size — approximately 25-35 KB. That's fine.\n\nAlso include \"project_number\": \"3489487\".\n\nApplicant address: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"].\n\nProduction locations as manufacturers:\n1. ABB Limited — [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n2. ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd. — [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District\", \"Shanghai, 201319\", \"P.R. China\"]\n3. ABB Inc. — [\"125 E. County Line Road\", \"Warminster, PA 18974\", \"United States of America\"]\n4. ABB India Limited — [\"Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area\", \"Bengaluru – 560058, Karnataka\", \"India\"]\n\nAlso include ABB Limited as applicant AND first manufacturer.\n\nOK, build the JSON.",
